const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The best career options after 12th humanities are law, design, a BA (Hons), integrated
    management, teaching, the Army wing of NDA, psychology, media and hospitality.</strong>
    Your smartest first move is to check which doors close first and which ones need maths. A humanities
    stream does not cap your income. Choosing a path with no proof of skill behind it does.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Career options after 12th humanities are wide, but the exam calendar decides which doors are still open. The CLAT registration window closes on 31 October 2026, and NID registration on 30 November 2026.</li>
      <li>Maths is the hidden gate: DU BA (Hons) Economics, the NDA written exam and IPMAT all need it, while most other humanities BA routes do not.</li>
      <li>Pay swings widely. A top National Law University reports a median above Rs 16 lakh a year for placed students, while typical entry roles sit far lower. Aim for a <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>, not a label, to unlock <strong>high income opportunities</strong> and move toward <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>.</li>
      <li>Shortlist two paths, run them through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol, and take one small real test of each before spending on coaching or fees.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    For the wider life-long picture of arts careers, see
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-in-arts/">career options in arts</a>. For the general
    map across all streams, see
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th/">career options after 12th</a>. If you want a
    second opinion on your own shortlist, our
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous
    career guidance</a> is built for exactly this decision.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="answer">The direct answer: choose by deadline, maths comfort and proof</h2>

  <p>
    Most guides on career options after 12th humanities give you a list of twenty jobs. That is not the
    hard part. The hard part is that your Class 12 result arrives, relatives start talking, and two or three
    of the best doors have already closed or are about to.
  </p>

  <p>
    A better order is the calendar first, then the maths gate, then the paths ranked by how far they can
    grow, then real pay with the number behind it.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Questions students actually ask</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"What are the best fields after humanities which have scope?"</li>
      <li>"If I choose arts, will I still get a good graduation and job later?"</li>
